//BUG="the type of an expression statement is never checked, so an impossible one reaches the C"
//ERR any

// `|x` dereferences `x`, and `x` is an i32, not a reference. When the result
// is used, that is caught: 07_references/52e_dereferencing_a_plain_value.cpl
// assigns it to a variable and gets an error.
//
// As a statement on its own it is not. The type resolver records that the
// expression has "one reference fewer than an i32" — a type that does not
// exist — but it only reports a type it is asked to produce, in
// `gather_types` (src/compiler/type_resolver/mod.rs), and it is only asked
// for the labels in `relevant_types`. `ASTStatement::Expression` in
// `normalize_block` (src/compiler/normalizer/mod.rs) throws the label of its
// expression away. So the statement is accepted and codegen emits
//
//     (*var0);
//
// which gcc rejects: "invalid type argument of unary '*' (have 'int')". The
// compiler reports success and leaves the user with an error in generated C.
//
// A stray `|` is an easy slip in a language where the same character begins
// every write through a reference. `| "text"` and `|f()` on a function that
// returns a value fail the same way.
//
// The fix is to keep the label of an expression statement among the types
// that must resolve. There is no `//MSG` because the diagnostic does not
// exist yet.

fn main
    x = 5
    |x
    out "ok"
